course: english
lesson: 24
title: Persuasive letters, newspaper articles and diaries
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A persuasive text tries to change your mind. «Dear Head Teacher, I am writing to ask for a longer lunch break. Firstly, children need time to eat. Secondly, exercise helps learning. Surely you agree?» It gives reasons in order (firstly, secondly) and ends with a question that doesn't need an answer, a rhetorical question, which makes the reader feel they should agree.
explain: Persuaders exaggerate. A leaflet says «Visit Sunnybay! The best beach in the world!» Mia asks, «Is it really the best?» Good question: nobody can prove it. «Everyone knows homework is pointless» sounds like a fact, but it is an opinion dressed up as one. Words like everyone, obviously and surely are clues.
explain: A newspaper article has a headline in big or capital letters to grab you and sum up the story: «LOCAL GIRL SAVES DUCKS». The first paragraph tells who, what, when and where: «Nine-year-old Ella Brown rescued six ducklings from a drain on Monday.» Quotes like «"It was terrifying," said Mrs Patel» give real people's words and make the report feel true. Look for a photo, a caption under it and columns too.
explain: A diary is personal and chatty: «Dear Diary, today was the WORST. I forgot my PE kit and Mr Hall made me wear spare shorts that were massive.» It uses «I», feelings, and contractions like «I'm» and «can't» that sound like talking. Dad in Liverpool says newspapers «always write it boss for their side, like» (boss means great): papers can lean towards one side, so comparing two reports of the same event is smart.
fact: Persuasive writing tries to make the reader agree or do something.
fact: Persuasive techniques include reasons in order, strong words, exaggeration and rhetorical questions.
fact: A rhetorical question is asked for effect and doesn't expect an answer, like «Surely you agree?»
fact: An opinion can be dressed up to sound like a fact, for example with «everyone knows».
fact: A headline is the title of a news story; it is short, bold and grabs attention.
fact: The first paragraph of a news article usually tells who, what, when and where.
fact: Newspaper articles include quotes, which are people's exact words in inverted commas.
fact: Newspaper features include a headline, a photo, a caption and columns.
fact: Newspapers can favour one side; comparing two reports helps you spot it.
fact: A diary is a personal record of events and feelings, written in the first person.
fact: Diaries often use informal language and contractions such as I'm, can't and didn't.
fact: Clues like candles, horses and carts, or old-fashioned words can show a diary is from long ago.
fact: Slang: «boss» (Liverpool) means great; «gutted» (Britain) means very disappointed; «ate» (Gen Z) means did brilliantly, as in «that letter ate».
exercise: «Firstly, children need time to eat. Secondly, exercise helps learning. Surely you agree?» Find a technique used to persuade. | A rhetorical question | A recipe | A diagram with labels, arrows and a key in the corner | A rhyme
exercise: Why is the headline «LOCAL GIRL SAVES DUCKS» in capitals? | To grab attention | Because the printer's keyboard was stuck on capital letters that morning | Because Ella shouted it | To make it a question
exercise: «Dear Diary, today was the WORST...» How do we know this is a diary? | It starts «Dear Diary» and tells personal feelings | It has a headline in capitals and a photograph with a caption underneath it | It has numbered steps | It gives facts about ducks
exercise: Why does an article include «"It was terrifying," said Mrs Patel»? | Real words make it feel true and alive | To fill up the space at the bottom of the page when the story runs out | Because Mrs Patel wrote it | To make it funny
exercise: The leaflet says «The best beach in the world!» Mia asks if it's really the best. What is true? | It's an exaggeration nobody can prove | It's a proven fact, because it's printed in a leaflet | It's a rhetorical question | It's a quote
exercise: Dad says newspapers «write it boss for their side». What does he mean? | Papers can make their own side look good | Papers are always completely fair to everybody they write about | Papers are run by bosses | Papers are always boring
exercise: Why does the diary use «I'm» and «can't»? | To sound chatty and personal | Because the writer was in a hurry and didn't have time to write the full words out | Because diaries must be formal | To make it look old
exercise: «Everyone knows homework is pointless.» Is this a fact? | No, it's an opinion dressed up as a fact | Yes, because it says everyone knows it | Yes, because it is in a letter | No, it's a question
exercise: Name two features of a newspaper article. | A headline and a caption | A glossary and numbered steps | Dear Diary and a signature | Rhymes and verses, like a song
exercise: Ella says the persuasive letter «ate». What does she mean? | It was brilliant | It was eaten | It was too short | It was boring
exercise: A diary says: «Mother lit the candle, and Father has taken the horse and cart to market.» How do we know it's from long ago? | Candles and a horse and cart for travel | It uses the word «Mother» | It is written in the first person, which only old diaries use | It has a date
exercise: What does the first paragraph of a news article usually tell? | Who, what, when and where | The ending of the story | The writer's favourite food | A joke to make you laugh before the serious part
exercise: What is a caption? | Words under a photo | The biggest headline | The name of the newspaper, printed at the very top of every page | A quote
exercise: Which word in a persuasive text is a clue to an opinion? | Obviously | Monday | Ducklings | Drain
test: «Surely you agree?» Does the writer want an actual answer? | No, it's a rhetorical question to push you to agree | Yes, the head must write back with an answer to the question | Yes, it's a quiz | No, it's a full stop
test: ★★ Which headline is best for an article about a dog that found a lost child? | HERO DOG FINDS LOST BOY | A dog found a little boy who had been lost for a while near the park on Tuesday | Dogs | Find out about dogs
test: ★★ Which line belongs in a diary, not a newspaper? | I was so embarrassed I wanted to hide | Nine-year-old Ella Brown rescued six ducklings | Police said the road will reopen on Friday | «It was terrifying,» said Mrs Patel
test: ★★★ A leaflet says: «Sunnybay has 3 cafés, a lifeguard and the best sand on Earth!» Which part can be checked as a fact? | It has 3 cafés and a lifeguard | It has the best sand on Earth | Every part of it | None of it, because leaflets never contain facts
test: ★★★ Paper A: «Brave Rovers fought hard but lost 2–1 to lucky City.» Paper B: «City deserved their 2–1 win over Rovers.» What is the one fact both agree on? | City won 2–1 | Rovers were brave | City were lucky | City deserved to win
test: «this letter ate, it's so convincing». What should you add to make this a good comment? | Which parts persuade and why | More slang | The writer's address | The date
test: In a cartoon, a newspaper headline says «MAN BITES DOG!» and everyone believes it at once. What should a careful reader do? | Read the article and check other reports | Believe all headlines | Ignore all newspapers forever | Only read the photos and the captions under them
test: What would happen to a newspaper article with no quotes from anyone? | It might feel less real and harder to trust | It would become a diary | It would be against the law | It would be longer
test: Mia writes a persuasive letter: «Give us a pet hamster. It's good.» How could she make it more persuasive? | Give reasons, like «it teaches us to care» | Write it all in capitals | Make it shorter still | Add «Dear Diary» at the start of the letter
test: «Dear Diary, I'm gutted, we lost again.» What does gutted mean? | Very disappointed | Very hungry | Very happy | Very tired
exam: Identify the persuasive device employed in the sentence «Surely you agree?» | A rhetorical question | A caption | A glossary entry | A numbered instruction of the kind found in a recipe
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the opening paragraph of a newspaper article? | It conveys who, what, when and where | It contains the moral of the story | It is invariably a quotation | It records the private feelings of the journalist concerning the events described
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the assertion «Everyone knows homework is pointless». | It is an opinion presented as though it were a fact | It is a verifiable fact | It is a rhetorical question | It is a headline
exam: Ascertain the feature by which a diary may most readily be distinguished from a newspaper report. | Its personal, first-person account of feelings | Its use of a headline | Its columns | Its quotations from witnesses who were present at the scene
